     rrppoorrtt — rdomain port interface pseudo-device

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppsseeuuddoo--ddeevviiccee rrppoorrtt

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The rrppoorrtt driver provides point-to-point interfaces for layer 3 connec‐
     tivity between rdomain(4) instances.

     Layer 3 connectivity between a pair of rdomains can be established by
     creating an rrppoorrtt interface in each rdomain, and connecting them together
     by configuring one of these interfaces as the parent of the other.

     rrppoorrtt interfaces can be created at runtime using the iiffccoonnffiigg rrppoorrtt_N
     ccrreeaattee command or by setting up a hostname.if(5) configuration file for
     netstart(8).  The interface itself can be configured with ifconfig(8);
     see its manual page for more information.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Create two rrppoorrtt interfaces in separate rdomain(4) instances and connect
     them together:

           # ifconfig rport0 create rdomain 0 up
           # ifconfig rport1 create rdomain 1 parent rport0 up
           # ifconfig rport0 inet 192.168.0.0/32 192.168.0.1
           # ifconfig rport1 inet 192.168.0.1/32 192.168.0.0

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     netintro(4), veb(4), hostname.if(5), pf.conf(5), ifconfig(8), netstart(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The rrppoorrtt driver first appeared in OpenBSD 7.6.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     David Gwynne <_d_l_g_@_o_p_e_n_b_s_d_._o_r_g>.

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
     A pair of rrppoorrtt interfaces must be created for each connection between a
     pair of rdomains.  Alternatively, if peering between multiple rdomain(4)
     instances is required, a single veb(4) interface with a vport(4) inter‐
     face for each rdomain can be used instead.

